that could be constrained if the government faces severe limits on its ability to borrow to fund operations."},{"industry":"health-providers","confidence":0.5,"rationale":"Hospitals and health systems receive significant federal reimbursements through Medicare and Medicaid, which could be affected by strict borrowing limits."},{"industry":"construction","confidence":0.4,"rationale":"Federal infrastructure and public-works spending, often financed through borrowing, would face new constraints under this amendment."}],"plainSummary":"This joint resolution proposes a constitutional amendment that would require a three-fourths vote in both the House and the Senate to authorize any increase in the federal debt for a specific purpose. Currently, Congress can raise the debt ceiling through ordinary legislation. The amendment would make it significantly harder for the government to borrow money by requiring an unusually high threshold of congressional approval.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"Anyone who pays tax or gets a federal payment.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"W000814","score":0.02831987198019769},"overlapCount":4},"meaning":{"percentile":37.76620918125887,"median":0.01073720309570498,"n":6339,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.7846913435605308}},{"billId":"119-hres-47","bioguideId":"H001096","cycle":2026,"score":0.005818742346366307,"matches":[{"industry":"education","donorAmount":7000,"donorShare":0.008525134135373893,"billConfidence":0.85,"contribution":0.005367677048198376},{"industry":"media-entertainment","donorAmount":1000,"donorShare":0.0012178763050534134,"billConfidence":0.5,"contribution":0.0004510652981679309}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.7846913435605308},"bill":{"id":"119-hres-47","congress":119,"billType":"hres","billNumber":"47","title":"Concerning the National Collegiate Athletic Association policy for eligibility in women's sports.","introducedDate":"2025-01-15","latestActionDate":"2025-01-15","latestActionText":"Referred to the House Committee on Education and Workforce.","policyArea":"Sports and Recreation","subjects":["Higher education","School athletics","Sex, gender, sexual orientation discrimination"],"sponsorBioguideId":"S001214","cosponsorCount":13,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-resolution/47","industries":[{"industry":"education","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"The resolution directly addresses NCAA policy, which governs collegiate athletics at member universities and affects their compliance obligations and program administration."},{"industry":"media-entertainment","confidence":0.5,"rationale":"Changes to NCAA eligibility rules could affect the composition and marketability of collegiate sports programming and events."}],"plainSummary":"This resolution calls on the National Collegiate Athletic Association to withdraw its current policy allowing transgender student-athletes to compete and instead adopt rules requiring participation in women's sports to be based on biological sex. The measure urges the NCAA to mandate that all member conferences implement such a policy across every sport and division. As a resolution, it expresses the position of the House but does not create enforceable law.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"College students, and their families.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"H001096","score":0.005818742346366307},"overlapCount":11},"meaning":{"percentile":27.938160593153494,"median":0.01073720309570498,"n":6339,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.7846913435605308}},{"billId":"119-hr-5880","bioguideId":"H001096","cycle":2026,"score":0.002466891492906488